<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Thames</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Thames is the longest river wholly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, flowing about 346 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotswolds">Cotswolds</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Since prehistoric times it has been a highway and a boundary, separating Celtic peoples and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and Mercia. London grew at its lowest bridging point, and its port made the city a world trading centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-thames.jpg" alt="River Thames" width="320" height="119" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:London_Thames_Sunset_panorama_-_Feb_2008.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archaeology and other sources suggest that the West Saxons, known early on as the Gewisse, formed first in the upper <b>Thames</b> valley around Dorchester-on-Thames, where early Anglo-Saxon cemeteries are numerous, and that groups in the Thames valley and the Solent region came together under a single dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After battles on the Medway and the <b>Thames</b>, Claudius himself arrived to enter Camulodunum (Colchester), the Catuvellaunian centre, and his triumphal arch in Rome recorded the surrender of eleven British kings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It may simply reflect how hard it was to bridge the <b>River Thames</b>: until the mid-18th century the Old London Bridge was the only crossing in London, its 19 narrow arches hampered the river, and it needed constant and costly repairs until a new bridge opened in 1831.</blockquote><a href="/songs/london-bridge-is-falling-down">London Bridge Is Falling Down</a></li><li><blockquote>Beyond this, nothing is recorded of their rulers, settlements or customs, and their territory is uncertain, though the upper <b>Thames</b> valley has been suggested.</blockquote><a href="/state/ancalites">Ancalites</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory is unknown; scholars have suggested places in the <b>Thames</b> valley or in Berkshire and Sussex, but no inscription or coin securely names them.</blockquote><a href="/state/bibroci">Bibroci</a></li><li><blockquote>Because their name resembles that of Cassivellaunus, some historians suggest they were his own people or a group within his following north of the <b>Thames</b>, perhaps in Hertfordshire, but there is no firm evidence.</blockquote><a href="/state/cassi">Cassi</a></li></ul></section>
